Grandfathering the buyback tax exemption for listed companies who had already announced buyback schemes before July 5, 2019 is a welcome clarification and should help listed companies that had not factored the buyback tax when the scheme was announced.

Summary of the corporate tax and MAT rate is provided below: * The companies can claim the concessional tax rate if the following exemptions/ incentives are not claimed by the company:

Deduction under section 10AA – SEZ tax holiday;

Additional depreciation under section 32(1)(iia);

Deduction under section 32AD (investment in new plant or machinery in notified backward areas in certain States), 33AB (Tea development account, coffee development account, and rubber development account) 3ABA (Site restoration fund);

Deduction under section 35(1)(ii) (amount paid to research association, institutions which has its object to undertake scientific research), 35(1)(iia) (amount paid to a company used by it for scientific research), 35(1)(iii) (amount paid to a research association, institutions which has its object to undertake research in social science or statistical research), 35(2AA) (amounts paid to National Laboratory, universities, IITs), 35(2AB) (in-house research expenses), 35AD (expenditure on specified business), 35CCC (expenses on agricultural extension project), 35CCD (expenditure on skill development project); or

Deduction under any provisions of Chapter VI-A under the heading "C-Deductions in respect of certain incomes" (income/ profit linked incentives) other than provisions of section 80JJAA (employment linked incentive)

Further, such companies will not be able to set-off any loss carried forward from any earlier assessment year if such loss is attributable to any of the deductions as referred above.

